## About this package

This package is part of the ${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION_MAJOR}.${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION_MINOR}.${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION_PATCH} release of Emgu LiteRT. It contains:

- `Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Extensions.AI.dll`: `LiteRtLmChatClient`, an `IChatClient` ([Microsoft.Extensions.AI](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/ai/microsoft-extensions-ai)) for the `${EMGU_LITERT_LM_MODELS_NUGET_ID}` language models, which run on the device through LiteRT-LM. Code written against `IChatClient` - including [Microsoft Agent Framework](https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/) agents - can use them like any other chat client: streaming, images and audio (`DataContent`, for models that accept them), thinking (`TextReasoningContent`, switched with `ChatOptions.Reasoning`) and tool calling (`UseFunctionInvocation()`, for models with `LanguageModel.SupportsToolCalling`: currently the Gemma 4 models).

```csharp
using Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Extensions.AI;
using Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Models;
using Microsoft.Extensions.AI;

using (Gemma4E2B model = new Gemma4E2B())
{
    await model.Init();
    using (IChatClient client = new ChatClientBuilder(model.AsIChatClient()).UseFunctionInvocation().Build())
    {
        ChatOptions options = new ChatOptions
        {
            Tools = new List<AITool> { AIFunctionFactory.Create(() => DateTime.Now.ToString("D"), "get_date", "Returns today's date") }
        };
        ChatResponse response = await client.GetResponseAsync("What day is it today?", options);
        Console.WriteLine(response.Text);
    }
}
```

`LiteRtLmChatClient` keeps one LiteRT-LM conversation open while each request only adds to it, so earlier turns aren't processed again. With tools, generation is constrained to well-formed calls of the given tools (`LiteRtLmChatClient.EnableConstrainedDecoding`, on by default; it needs a LiteRT-LM v0.18.0 or later native library and is skipped with an older one). Tool calling uses `UseFunctionInvocation()` from the `Microsoft.Extensions.AI` package.

## Context budgeting for tool loops

The MAUI demo enables boundary context management; package callers opt in explicitly:

```csharp
var native = new LiteRtLmChatClient(model) {
    EnableContextManagement = true,
    MaxThinkingTokens = 256
};
native.ContextChanged += info => Console.WriteLine(info); // counts only; no prompt or reasoning text
using var client = new ChatClientBuilder(native).UseFunctionInvocation().Build();
```

The client reads the loaded engine's configured capacity (including `configureSettings` overrides), the native
KV-cache count (using a conservative 4096 capacity when native settings leave it unspecified), the rendered prompt preface (instructions, schemas and history) and estimates pending payload/media
costs. JSON transport escaping is not counted as model text. It reserves 64 tokens of safety headroom and sets
per-message output/thinking budgets. Thinking remains enabled when requested, with at most `MaxThinkingTokens`
(default 256), further reduced to fit that round. Explicit `ChatOptions.MaxOutputTokens` remains an upper bound.

At a model/tool boundary with insufficient headroom, the client rebuilds the native conversation without completed
reasoning. It retains the latest original user request and every associated tool call/result, including media tool
results. Complete older exchanges can be dropped if needed. This process never executes tools; already completed
side effects are not replayed. Rebuilding requires prefilling retained text again, so it can increase latency.
The host can still summarize older exchanges before a send, as the MAUI demo does.

If essential current content cannot fit, `LiteRtLmContextException` gives actionable capacity guidance. Empty
completions are not silently treated as successful answers when management is enabled. `ChatFinishReason.Length`
is reported when native cache or available benchmark decode counts show a limit was reached. Native APIs do not
always expose a distinct stop reason; diagnostics distinguish empty output when no limit can be established.
Set `EngineSettings.EnableBenchmark()` at model initialization to include native per-round decode counts.
Estimates and media costs can differ from actual native token use; capacity errors remain possible and are surfaced
without automatically restarting the whole agent request. Do not add a host retry that replays tool side effects.

